From: Trygve Bauge <trygveb@lightning.powertech.no>
Date: Thu, 8 Dec 1994 10:07:13 +6000
Subject: [#94-12-320] Privatization of National Parks.

On Wed, 7 Dec 1994, Phil Goetz wrote:

> On Wed, 7 Dec 1994, Trygve Bauge wrote:
> >As long as clear cutting, tree planting and managed cultivation
> >go hand in hand there isn't much of a problem.
> > 
> >And let us not forget that areas can be revegetated.
> 
> Let us not forget that old-growth forests take hundreds of years to
> develop, and you will not have any with this method.
> It's not enough just to have "trees".  They're not all the same.
> (This reminds me of a Doonesbury comic, where Trudeau had James Watt
> say that concerns about endangered birds were silly because they
> still had lots of pigeons.)

In areas with a lot of rain- like in Norway- you get tall trees
in less than 100 years. Which means that you have a lot of full
grown trees in a well managed forest.

Intelligent forestry consists of planting a variety of trees,
and letting different parts of the forest have different age.
Crop rotation and different aging give a steady supply of
harvestable trees, and also enables fire barriers and a thriving biotop
which makes it easier to fend off diseases and insect attacks,
When some areas are clear cut you also get berries, and a varried forest 
with open areas with sun and view, that are pleasing to walk and ski in.

When it comes to the rational managing of a forest,
both clear cutting and the cutting of individual trees
are management tools that it is useful to apply to parts of the forest,
so to best maintain the latter.

With the growing interest for unmanaged forests. I don't see why some
enterprising private entrepreneur couldn't buy a forest, and sell
tickets for people to walk through it. A group of people could
also form a company, sell shares, and buy up a forest and close it for
human access alltogether (even when it burns down, as it certainly will
if it is not being managed.)

> 
> Also note that when preservation is by private individuals, each person
> can at any time decide to "develop" whatever resource he's got.  For a
> redwood forest to grow undisturbed for 500 years, you would need every
> individual who ever owned that forest to decide every year for 500 years
> that it was more advantageous to keep it uncut.  Just one swing in the
> market in 500 years that makes cutting profitable is all it takes to
> undercut hundreds of years of conservation.
> 
A potential dollar profit doesn't mean that something will be done!
People have all kind of motivations. Personally I meassure profit
not in dollars but in life extension, and a healthy environment
is an important means. Dollar profit is important too, but only 
as long as it doesn't violate the most life extending path.

In Europe there are still lots of private buildings around
that are a thousand years old or older, churches, castles,
old city buildings etc. There are also lots of old trees
in the center of private yards. Though now often protected by the government,
these houses and trees were maintained for centuries without being 
"protected" by government decree. 

Which goes to show that private individuals can maintain
values too, probably more so than governments can.

(E.g. a government that can protect an area, can also unprotect it,
when national concerns or economic conserns take presedence.)
And wars fought among governments are still the main destroyers
of both wilderness and developed areas, whether protected or not.
 
I like old redwoods myself.
And I would regard these as much safer if individual environmentalists
owned these redwoods, than if the government owned them.

The government can always be pressured into changing its 
national parks. Does anyone really think that a national park is protected
if it suddenly was discovered that the only main domestic source
of some rare strategically important mineral was within that national park? 

Respect for private property is the best defense against
governments and majorities that want to develop an area.

