From: Anders Sandberg <nv91-asa@nada.kth.se>
Date: Thu, 8 Dec 1994 12:23:34 +0100 (MET)
Subject: [#94-12-322] UPLOADS: Ain't no singularity

I wrote:

> I think the computing needs of the VR environments they will live in 
> are of no importance, compared to the inhabitants they are nothing more 
> than screen savers.

Jordan Sparks responded:

>Wrong.  Even the most advanced supercomputers today cannot even come 
>close to simulating reality.  In spite of the claims of the popular 
>press, VR is decades away. 

How many decades away do you think *uploading* is? :-)

>Now, if you increase the speed of the human's 
>mind (say, double it), the VR has to keep up by doubling its speed.  The 
>relationship between the speed of the VR and the human brain is a 
>constant, no matter what the factor of increase in speed up.  

Yes, this is true. however, lets compare the complexity of a full VR
environment with some reasonable optimization, and an brute-force uploaded
mind. The mind is essentially a simulation of 10^11 neurons and their
10^15 synapses, running with a time subdivision less than a microsecond
(subjective time). Lets say each synapse requires around a kilobyte of
data, then we get on the order of 10^15 kilobytes of information in the
brain simulation. Other interactions are probably important, but doesn't
increase the size with more than one order or so, so we get a simulation
size of 10^16 kilobytes, running at 1kHz (compared to a subjective time). 

The VR environment is essentially a very complex body-simulation linked to
a huge database containing the "world". I read somewhere that to appear
realistic, a VR had to render 80 million polygons/objects each frame
(which can be around 100-50 Hz), but this can probably be done easier by
raytracing from the retina (which means we need just care about the
quality of observed objects). We also have to simulate touch, hearing,
physics etc to make the VR realistic. This is awesomely complex, but
doesn't require as much detail as the brain simulation (we simulate the
appearance, texture and mechanics of piece of a table, not the physics of
every fibre inside). If we assume each object requires around a kilobyte
of data, we get around 10^10 kilobytes of information in this VR
simulation. This is 10^-6 of the mind simulation, and it doesn't have to
be updated as fast in most cases, so there is another time factor of 10 or
so. 

The body simulation is probably very computationally expensive, but since
we hardly simulate each cell, we can get away with maybe around 10^9-10^10
"objects", each requiring perhaps a megabyte of information (we will
probably not strive for total realism, but to make everything "feel right"
for the upload). This will require 10^13 kilobytes of information, updated
at neural frequency. This is still a factor of 10^-3 smaller than the
brain simulation. 

So we see that the ratio of upload/environment is around 10^3 in this
gross estimate. The computational demands are of course titanic, but if we
can get an upload to work we can probably get the VR to work too. 


>VR is more than a screen saver now, and will always be more.

After seeing how screen savers eat CPU time, I doubt a full VR would be
that different. :-)

