Subject: [#94-12-326] BASICS: Old-Growth Forestry

On Thu, 8 Dec 1994 nancy@genie.slhs.udel.edu wrote:

>  Tim Starr <timstarr@netcom.com> wrote: 
> >If you don't "manage" old forests, they eventually all die off at once.
> >It may take a long time, but they all go together when they go.  Then
> >you don't have any old trees left, whereas if you regularly cut down
> >parts and let new trees grow back, then you might have a more sustainable
> >preserve.
> 
> Could you cite some examples of this happening? I'm not saying that it's
> impossible, but I've never heard of it before.
> 

Whole forests die in fires, (Yellowstone a few years ago)
Whole forests of older weaker trees die from insect attacks
we had that happen to spruce trees(?) in Colorado a few years ago.
Strands of larger trees blow over in wind storms or break in avalances.
Trees are prone to yeast attack (?) and other diseases, lightning strikes, 
attaks by animals (not just insects). Some trees or their bark are eaten 
by insects or animals. Early frost and early snow falls also take their
toll, as do droughts.

Trees and whole groups of trees, die, for a variety of reasons.

Having bio diversity and trees of all ages give some protection
against all the trees dying out at the same time and of the same cause.

Redwoods are just a little more long lived, than most trees.
On the other hand we do not have old redwoods all over the place,
which means that the environment must have something to say
as to where redwoods are likely to survive and why.

Even the oldest redwoods are only so many thousand years old.

In conclusion: all old trees eventually die of natural causes
in the wild.

On the other hand, maybe we could learn from the redwoods,
recreate the conditions under which these have survived and manage a 
forest so that a forest of trees are kept alive for ever and ever??

Managed forests: the best way to create old growth forests!

The forest in Yellowstone would have been older if it had been
managed, instead it was unmanaged and burned down!

On the other hand: With very few exceptions old forests are not a 
desireable goal.

For hiking purposes and estetic experience a varied forest is
much more exiting than miles and miles of old trees and rotten wood.

On the one hand it is inspiring and intriguing to come across
one really old and weather bitten tree, and it is worth saving a few such
here and there.

On the other hand:
once a forest is 50 or 60 years old the trees are already tall,
and once it is 100 years old there are enough dead trees around to
house all animals and insects usually found in even older forests,
thus we do not really need any forest older than that to maintain
the spotted owl etc.

It is sound management to recycle a forest so that the whole forest
is cut and replanted or reseeded over a 100 year period,
take or leave a few years based on growth rate (altitude, temperature
and rain).

In some climates clear cutting is o.k.
while in dryer and colder climates we have to maintain trees of all 
ages, and only harvest individual trees so to leave enough trees 
behind to maintain enough weather protection, evaporation and rain 
induction, for young trees to grow up.

